    "title": "Sudoku Solver: Problem #50 (Goldman Sachs Placement Classic)",
    "description": "Given the placement scenario at Goldman Sachs, solve the following Sudoku Solver problem:\n\nYou are given an input sequence representing transactional or relational data. Implement an optimal algorithmic function to evaluate the sudoku solver criteria, handling all boundary edge cases.\n\nExample 1:\nInput: data = [2, 7, 11, 15], target = 9\nOutput: [0, 1]\nExplanation: Indices 0 and 1 sum to 9.\n\nConstraints:\n- 1 <= N <= 2 * 10^5\n- Time Limit: 1.0 second\n- Memory Limit: 256 MB",
    "topic": "Backtracking",
    "subtopic": "Sudoku Solver",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "pattern": "Sudoku Solver",
    "platform": "GeeksforGeeks",
    "platformUrl": "https://geeksforgeeks.com/problems/sudoku-solver-50",
    "company": "Goldman Sachs",
    "expectedTime": "30 mins",
    "hints": [
      "Understand the core properties of Backtracking: consider what information can be precomputed or tracked.",
      "For Sudoku Solver, avoid the naive nested loop approach. Can you use a hash map, two pointers, or a heap to reduce operations?",
      "Apply the Sudoku Solver pattern: maintain invariant state and update answers dynamically in a single pass.",
      "Pseudocode: Initialize data structure -> Loop through input -> Check condition -> Update optimal state -> Return result."
    ],
    "bruteForce": "Exhaustive search over all possible pairs/subarrays. Time: O(N^2), Space: O(1).",
    "betterApproach": "Sorting the array or using a temporary data structure. Time: O(N log N), Space: O(N).",
    "optimalApproach": "Single-pass using the optimal Sudoku Solver technique. Time: O(N), Space: O(1) or O(N).",
    "pythonSolution": "# Python 3 Solution - Sudoku Solver: Problem #50 (Goldman Sachs Placement Classic)\ndef solve(data, target=None):\n    # Optimal Sudoku Solver approach\n    seen = {}\n    for i, val in enumerate(data):\n        complement = (target - val) if target is not None else val\n        if complement in seen:\n            return [seen[complement], i]\n        seen[val] = i\n    return [-1, -1]\n",
    "cppSolution": "// C++ Solution - Sudoku Solver: Problem #50 (Goldman Sachs Placement Classic)\n#include <vector>\n#include <unordered_map>\nusing namespace std;\n\nclass Solution {\npublic:\n    vector<int> solve(const vector<int>& data, int target = 0) {\n        unordered_map<int, int> seen;\n        for (int i = 0; i < data.size(); ++i) {\n            int complement = target - data[i];\n            if (seen.find(complement) != seen.end()) {\n                return {seen[complement], i};\n            }\n            seen[data[i]] = i;\n        }\n        return {-1, -1};\n    }\n};\n",
    "javaSolution": "// Java Solution - Sudoku Solver: Problem #50 (Goldman Sachs Placement Classic)\nimport java.util.HashMap;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\npublic class Solution {\n    public int[] solve(int[] data, int target) {\n        Map<Integer, Integer> seen = new HashMap<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < data.length; i++) {\n            int complement = target - data[i];\n            if (seen.containsKey(complement)) {\n                return new int[]{seen.get(complement), i};\n            }\n            seen.put(data[i], i);\n        }\n        return new int[]{-1, -1};\n    }\n}\n",
    "timeComplexity": "O(N)",
